Verify Helm is installed in your Codespaces terminal (Minikube / Codespaces typically includes it, 
or we install it via script):

helm version --short

If it returns a version (e.g., v3.x.x), you are ready. If not, install it with one command:

curl https://raw.githubusercontent.com/helm/helm/main/scripts/get-helm-3 | bash

Step 1: Create a Boilerplate Chart
Run:

helm create web-suite

Inspect the generated folder:

ls -la web-suite
ls -la web-suite/templates

Step 2: Dry-Run and Template Rendering
Before deploying anything to your cluster, render the templates locally to see the raw YAML that Helm would produce:

helm template my-release ./web-suite

 path: /
              port: http
---
# Source: web-suite/templates/tests/test-connection.yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: "my-release-web-suite-test-connection"
  labels:
    helm.sh/chart: web-suite-0.1.0
    app.kubernetes.io/name: web-suite
    app.kubernetes.io/instance: my-release
    app.kubernetes.io/version: "1.16.0"
    app.kubernetes.io/managed-by: Helm
  annotations:
    "helm.sh/hook": test
spec:
  containers:
    - name: wget
      image: busybox
      command: ['wget']
      args: ['my-release-web-suite:80']
  restartPolicy: Never

Notice how Helm took default variables from values.yaml and injected them into the manifests inside templates/.


Step 3: Install the Chart onto Your Cluster
Deploy the chart as a release named dev-webb:

helm install dev-webb ./web-suite

